WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.480
This is the scariest chart in electrical&nbsp;
engineering. It puts the fear of God into

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.120
all undergraduate students. First time that you&nbsp;
ever laid eyes on this chart. What was your first

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.800
impression? Holy. It's like terrifying. It&nbsp;
looks almost like a wormhole in some sci-fi

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.960
movie. I mean, on many versions, it literally&nbsp;
says black magic. But for all the fear, it's

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.840
everywhere. Millions of copies have been printed,&nbsp;
and it's built into the most advanced software we

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.640
have today. That's because this chart solves one&nbsp;
of the most paradoxical problems in electrical

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.920
engineering. So, we put it to the test. This is&nbsp;
crazy. We're getting way more power transfer,

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.120
even though we've cut the line. How does that&nbsp;
make any sense? There have been some great

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.600
YouTube videos explaining how to use it, like&nbsp;
one from my friend Zach Star. But I always felt

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.000
like they were missing something essential. You&nbsp;
also, you want to know why this chart looks the

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.200
way it does? Yes. But then I need to know the&nbsp;
physics. I would want to know what the circles

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.200
are and what I'm looking at at first because&nbsp;
even once you know how to use this chart,

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.320
you're still left wondering how it pulls off its&nbsp;
strangest bit of witchcraft, trapping infinity

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.000
inside a finite circle. Well, to understand that,&nbsp;
we have to go back a hundred years to the origins

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.960
of this chart. In 1928, Phillip H. Smith,&nbsp;
fresh out of electrical engineering school,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:20.320
got his first job at Bell Labs. The telephone&nbsp;
industry was booming. Americans were placing

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.680
more than 65 million calls a day. But because&nbsp;
telephone calls had to go through copper cables,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.160
every call had a limit, the coast. At the time, no&nbsp;
telephone cables crossed the Atlantic. So the only

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:37.920
way to call across continents was by using radio&nbsp;
waves. Smith began work on a mission to send radio

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.560
signals from New Jersey to receiving stations&nbsp;
thousands of kilometers away. One in England

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:48.880
and one in Argentina. With only one antenna, the&nbsp;
signal radiates across the whole sky, and only a

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.160
small portion of the power reaches the receiver.&nbsp;
But with two antennas, the waves combine. In some

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.160
directions, they cancel each other out, and in&nbsp;
others, they reinforce. So, right here in the

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.120
middle, the power doubles. If you add even more&nbsp;
antennas, it narrows the beam further. Focus it to

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.880
about 10° across, and the power in that direction&nbsp;
is 400 times stronger. So, to have any chance of

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.280
their signal reaching across the globe, Smith's&nbsp;
team connected more than 20 smaller antennas into

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:19.200
a massive directional array linked by over 2 km&nbsp;
of transmission line. Smith's job was to test this

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.840
massive array. But when he tried sending a signal&nbsp;
from the source down the line to an antenna, well,

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.120
he noticed something strange. Part of it was&nbsp;
bouncing back. And that reflection meant that

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.840
a large portion of the power never reached the&nbsp;
antenna. If he wanted to send a signal across the

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.560
planet, he needed to make sure that all the power&nbsp;
reached the emitting antennas. So, he needed to

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.640
find a way to minimize the reflections. Now, the&nbsp;
reason Smith was getting these reflections is that

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.160
he wasn't working with the kind of electricity&nbsp;
that we might be used to, like with a battery and

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.120
a light bulb. Here the battery provides a constant&nbsp;
voltage which drives a steady current through the

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.480
wire which lights up the bulb. This is direct&nbsp;
current or DC. But to generate radio signals,

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.200
electrons have to oscillate back and forth at&nbsp;
radio frequency. As they accelerate, they create

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.960
changing electromagnetic fields that propagate&nbsp;
out as radio waves. So the voltage has to rise

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:15.600
and fall and reverse direction and the current&nbsp;
has to as well. That's alternating current or AC.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.320
In the simplest case, both the voltage and the&nbsp;
current are smooth sine waves. Now, in reality,

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:24.720
it does get a bit more complicated than that, but&nbsp;
for now, let's just look at a single sinusoidal

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:31.360
voltage and current. Let's recreate Smith's signal&nbsp;
by shaking a slinky up and down. You can think of

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.880
my arm as the power source. So, waves travel down&nbsp;
the line to the other end. And you can see that

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:41.200
they have some key characteristics. The distance&nbsp;
from one peak to another or from one valley to

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:46.400
another is consistent. This is the wavelength&nbsp;
and how many peaks pass a point per second is

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.040
the frequency of the wave. So if you know the&nbsp;
number of peaks that go by and you know the

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.840
distance between those peaks, well by multiplying&nbsp;
the two together, you get the speed of the wave.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.400
The formula is that the speed is equal to the&nbsp;
wavelength times the frequency. In Smith's case,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.200
he needed to emit radio waves and they sit on the&nbsp;
electromagnetic spectrum alongside microwaves,

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.480
infrared, and visible light. And light waves all&nbsp;
travel at the same speed in a vacuum, about 300

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.560
million m/s or c. In a transmission line, the&nbsp;
wave travels more slowly, but for a given line,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.520
that speed is still fixed. And if the speed is&nbsp;
fixed, well, that means that frequency must be

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:29.280
inversely proportional to wavelength. Let&nbsp;
me show you. If you decrease the frequency,

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.280
the wavelength gets longer. And if you&nbsp;
increase it, the wavelength gets shorter.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.880
And this wavelength is crucial to our problem.&nbsp;
We'll also show you in simulation here without

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:44.800
the effects of friction and damping. See,&nbsp;
if we start out with a long wavelength,

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.360
the signal barely changes during the time it&nbsp;
takes to travel down the slinky. Once the wave

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.920
reaches the other fixed end, any reflection that&nbsp;
comes back looks almost identical to the original

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.480
wave. The resulting interference of these two&nbsp;
waves doesn't change the overall shape of the

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:03.840
original signal all that much. So, there's nothing&nbsp;
to worry about. But when I increase the frequency

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.320
to the point where the wave becomes shorter than&nbsp;
the slinky itself. Well, now when the wave reaches

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:15.840
the fixed end, the reflection back interferes with&nbsp;
the incoming wave. After a reflection, the forward

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.680
and reflected waves combine. At some points,&nbsp;
the two waves cancel and the voltage is very

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:29.280
small. And at other points, they add together.&nbsp;
This is what's called a standing wave pattern.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:36.400
In electrical systems, the standing wave pattern&nbsp;
can be a big problem. That's because if the

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.400
reflections in your transmission line are bad&nbsp;
enough, the peak voltages can reach up to twice

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:46.240
the input voltage. And if your line isn't rated&nbsp;
for that, well, it burns out. There was a huge

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:53.840
reflection and powerful standing wave did this&nbsp;
to the inner conductor of that transmission line.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:06:00.320
This is the effect of the standing wave. It's not&nbsp;
made up. In most electrical systems we're used to,

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.960
this isn't too much of a problem. Your house&nbsp;
supplies AC power at a frequency of 50 or 60

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:11.040
Hz. So that corresponds to a wavelength of about&nbsp;
5 to 6,000 km, which is far longer than any wire

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:16.480
that takes power from the station to your home.&nbsp;
But Smith was generating radio waves in the MHz

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.600
range. And at 10 MHz, the wavelength is about&nbsp;
30 m. With the transmission line he was working

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:27.440
on more than 2 km long, well, that's many times&nbsp;
the wavelength. So reflections were significant.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.240
That's why we went to Imperial College London's&nbsp;
screened radio frequency anechoic chamber to see

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.120
if we could recreate what Smith was up against. As&nbsp;
you can see, it's very small. It's very compact,

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:42.000
but we can do a lot of antenna measurements in&nbsp;
here. We can do electromagnetic compatibility

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.440
measurements here. Here's the experiment. We've&nbsp;
got a power source sending a radio signal down a

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.920
transmission line to an antenna array, and we put&nbsp;
it in an anechoic chamber to reduce interference.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.520
We've got a receiving antenna at the other end&nbsp;
which is able to pick up the signal and tell

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.360
us just how much power is actually making&nbsp;
it to our antennas. It's a scaled model of

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.240
Smith's configuration. Now, based on our input&nbsp;
power and the distance between the antennas,

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:11.440
we expect a signal of about -55 dB. But that's&nbsp;
not what we see. We've lost half the power.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:16.800
We're picking up around -59 dB. And because&nbsp;
signal is measured on a logarithmic scale,

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:22.560
that 4-decibel drop means we've lost more than&nbsp;
half the power, exactly what was happening to

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.120
Smith. So let's find out what's stealing it.&nbsp;
To do that, let's simplify this like a true

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.320
engineer. We can model the electrical signal as&nbsp;
a simple sinusoidal voltage and current source

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.040
which we draw like this. Next is the transmission&nbsp;
line. It consists of two conductors that form a

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:42.560
loop. At the far end is the antenna and its exact&nbsp;
design varies. So for now we'll just represent it

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:47.360
with a black box. That gives us a very simple&nbsp;
toy model with just three parts. And we can

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:52.320
represent these parts with slinkies. Now, so&nbsp;
far we've used just one slinky with one set of

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.600
properties to show how waves travel. But the real&nbsp;
setup is more than just the transmission line.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.200
Since both the antenna and the transmission&nbsp;
line have their own electrical properties,

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:06.000
we can model Smith's whole system by tying two&nbsp;
slinkies together. One for the transmission line

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.720
and the other for the antenna. First, we're&nbsp;
going to try two very different slinkies. One

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:17.280
with a high mass per unit length and one with a&nbsp;
low mass per unit length. Let's see what happens.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:22.720
Well, when we send a pulse down&nbsp;
the line, it reaches the boundary

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:29.280
between the two slinkies. Part&nbsp;
of the energy continues through

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:34.560
and part of it reflects back. The severity of&nbsp;
the reflection is captured by a ratio called the

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.360
reflection coefficient. Its magnitude is just the&nbsp;
size of the reflected wave divided by the forward

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.720
wave. But if the two slinkies have the same mass&nbsp;
per unit length, the wave passes straight through.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:48.960
The reflection coefficient is then zero. So when&nbsp;
there's a big mismatch in mass per unit length,

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.760
we get large reflections. But when the properties&nbsp;
match, the reflections disappear. That's because

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:59.200
how the wave actually travels through the slinky&nbsp;
depends on two properties. How tightly the slinky

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.680
is stretched and this mass per unit length.&nbsp;
But since in this setup, both slinkies are

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:07.840
tied together, they're under the same tension. So&nbsp;
then the only difference is their mass per unit

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:12.720
length. If we can match this property, it's like&nbsp;
the discontinuity is gone and it's just one slinky

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.840
again. So, you might think Smith could do the same&nbsp;
thing, except instead of matching slinkies, he had

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:23.200
to match his transmission line to his antenna.&nbsp;
But what electrical property could do that? Well,

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.520
the mass per unit length decides how easy it&nbsp;
is for a wave to travel through our slinky. So,

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:32.080
an analogous version for our electrical system&nbsp;
might just be its resistance. Because the higher

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.720
the resistance, the harder it is for current&nbsp;
to flow. That's Ohm's law. The resistance is

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:41.520
equal to the voltage divided by the current. So&nbsp;
if a voltage wave hits the boundary between the

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.600
transmission line and the antenna and the&nbsp;
resistance changes across that boundary,

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:49.760
well the whole wave can't pass through. Some&nbsp;
of it reflects just like what we saw with the

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:55.520
slinkies. Indeed in real life when we checked the&nbsp;
resistances didn't match. So the antenna array is

00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:00.400
at 12 1/2 ohms and the transmission line is at&nbsp;
50. So what we're going to do is we're going to

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:05.440
add this 37 1/2 ohm or 40 ohm resistor to try to&nbsp;
bring it up so that they match and we'll see what

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:11.280
happens to the power. Okay, what are we looking&nbsp;
So now it's uh yeah, it hasn't helped us at all.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:16.480
It's actually Yeah, we're at half the power.&nbsp;
Interesting. But why is that? Resistors have a

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:22.000
problem. They work by taking power and dissipating&nbsp;
it through heat. So they're fundamentally lossy.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:26.000
That means by putting it in our system here,&nbsp;
we're going to lose energy no matter what. But

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.280
there's another problem with using resistors,&nbsp;
and that's that real life systems don't just

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:35.440
have resistances. Take this disposable camera.&nbsp;
When I turn on the flash, it takes a moment to

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:43.600
charge. You can hear it. Then when I take a&nbsp;
picture, it releases all that energy at once.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:48.160
That's because inside are two conductive plates&nbsp;
separated by a gap. When I turn on the flash,

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:52.560
I apply a voltage across them. And because they're&nbsp;
not connected, charge builds up on each side,

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:58.400
storing energy. This forms a capacitor, and its&nbsp;
ability to store charge is called its capacitance.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:02.640
When I take the picture, the circuit closes,&nbsp;
and that stored energy is released all at once,

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.520
which produces the flash. But this isn't&nbsp;
just in cameras. A transmission line has

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:12.800
two conductors held a distance apart. So it has&nbsp;
a capacitance as well, but it uses alternating

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:16.880
current. So let's see what happens when we have&nbsp;
a capacitor with an alternating current signal.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:23.920
Okay. So we're going to make a chart and on&nbsp;
the horizontal axis I'm going to draw time.

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:29.360
And on the vertical axis we'll&nbsp;
just have magnitude of our wave.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:34.480
Okay. So we'll start with our voltage&nbsp;
wave which we'll just draw like this.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:42.000
Now we'll draw a current wave in red.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.720
As the voltage rises, charge builds up. At&nbsp;
the peak voltage, that buildup stops. And

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:50.960
since current is the flow of charge, when the&nbsp;
charges stop moving, at that point the current

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:55.520
is zero. Then as the voltage falls, the charge&nbsp;
flows back. And that's when the current is the

00:11:55.520 --> 00:12:01.280
largest. So unlike resistance, voltage and current&nbsp;
don't peak at the same time. Since the horizontal

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:06.160
axis is time, this shows the current peaking&nbsp;
before the voltage, always by the same amount,

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:13.200
a quarter of a cycle. In other words, the voltage&nbsp;
lags the current by 90°. But there is another

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:17.040
effect we need to take into account. That's&nbsp;
because whenever current flows through a wire,

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:22.480
it creates a magnetic field. And if you coil that&nbsp;
wire many times over, the magnetic field from each

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:26.960
loop adds together, which makes the effect much&nbsp;
stronger. That's why in circuits, you might often

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:31.840
see components that look like this. But even&nbsp;
on a straight wire like our transmission line,

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:36.800
these fields still exist and they affect the&nbsp;
way that our current and voltage waves behave.

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:41.680
A changing magnetic field induces a voltage&nbsp;
that opposes any change in the current. And the

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:46.720
faster you try to change that current, the more&nbsp;
voltage pushes back. When the current is at zero,

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:51.760
the slope of the curve is at its steepest. So&nbsp;
the current is changing the fastest. Therefore,

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:56.560
the voltage is at its peak. Further along&nbsp;
the cycle, the current reaches its maximum.

00:12:56.560 --> 00:13:01.200
For just an instant, it has stopped changing. So&nbsp;
the slope is flat, and that's when voltage drops

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:07.280
to zero. Past the peak, the current begins to&nbsp;
fall and the voltage flips direction. So again,

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:13.040
the voltage and current peaks don't line up.&nbsp;
The voltage leads the current by 90°. So that's

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:18.160
why matching resistances alone wasn't enough in&nbsp;
our demo. With resistance, we can only describe

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:23.120
the relative size of our voltage and current&nbsp;
waves or the magnitude. But it says nothing

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:28.000
about their timing. Capacitance and inductance&nbsp;
shift the current wave relative to the voltage

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:33.600
wave. That is they shift the phase. So a real&nbsp;
