00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:39.600
We have to match the magnitude and the phase. Now&nbsp;
one common way to represent something with both a

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:44.720
magnitude and a phase is to use complex numbers.&nbsp;
They consist of two parts. A real part represented

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:52.320
on the x-axis and an imaginary part represented&nbsp;
on the y-axis. This is the complex plane. Now,

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:56.240
because electrical engineers use I to refer to&nbsp;
current, we're going to refer to imaginary numbers

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:02.000
with a J. And just to shake things up even more,&nbsp;
most electrical engineering textbooks swap the

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:07.600
