00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:23.520
a bit of extra transmission line called a stub.&nbsp;
We've connected one branch to the inner cable of

00:32:23.520 --> 00:32:28.400
the stub, one branch to the outer. And since at&nbsp;
the end of our stub, the inner wire and the outer

00:32:28.400 --> 00:32:33.280
shield aren't actually connected, this means we've&nbsp;
created a break in our circuit. On the Smith chart

00:32:33.280 --> 00:32:38.240
that means we know that the end of the stub lies&nbsp;
here at the open circuit position. But the rest

00:32:38.240 --> 00:32:42.720
of the circuit doesn't see the far end directly.&nbsp;
It sees the impedance at the input of the stub,

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:48.240
which is this connection point. And that depends&nbsp;
on how long the stub is. Think of the stub as a

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:53.120
little cove branching off a river. Water runs&nbsp;
in, bounces off the back wall, and flows out

00:32:53.120 --> 00:32:58.080
again. And the length of the cove sets the timing&nbsp;
of that returning water. Size the cove right,

00:32:58.080 --> 00:33:02.480
and the water flowing out pushes exactly when&nbsp;
you want it to. And that's what the stub's length

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:09.280
does. It sets the timing of the wave that reflects&nbsp;
back. We want it to equal 1.8 positive. So,

00:33:09.280 --> 00:33:16.480
we start at where the open circuit is&nbsp;
and walk all the way around our chart.

00:33:16.480 --> 00:33:25.840
And we stop at 1.8. When we measure the angle, we&nbsp;
see that that's about 302°. Okay. Now, 302 / 360,

00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:30.080
that gives us how far along we've been on the&nbsp;
circle. And we know that one full circle turn

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:35.280
gives us half the wavelength. We get 77 mm.&nbsp;
That should be the length of our stub. Now,

00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:38.880
that's the shortest length that works. But&nbsp;
since the chart repeats every half wavelength,

00:33:38.880 --> 00:33:44.960
we could add another full lap, 92 mm, and reach&nbsp;
exactly the same reactance. We're going to start

00:33:44.960 --> 00:33:49.520
with a longer stub and then trim it down to try&nbsp;
and tune our match. We haven't matched it yet,

00:33:49.520 --> 00:33:52.640
so we're still we still have some&nbsp;
of this reactance that we have to

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:57.280
cancel out. Exactly. And hopefully as we cut&nbsp;
this transmission line shorter and shorter,

00:33:57.280 --> 00:34:00.880
we'll move through that point. All right. All&nbsp;
right. So, let's do it. Let's do it. You tell

00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:08.880
tell me what's happening. Okay. Just like a&nbsp;
little bit basically like that might be. Come

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:17.840
on. Give me that. Ooh, that's not too bad.&nbsp;
Yes. All right. Here's the last little bit.

00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:23.040
That's not bad. That's a good match. That's&nbsp;
a good match. Okay. That's awesome. It's

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:28.160
actually so weird. Literally by just changing&nbsp;
the length of this line here, this this stub,

00:34:28.160 --> 00:34:33.840
we've matched the impedance. Insane. Look.&nbsp;
Look at it. We stopped every reflection on

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:38.480
this line with just a break in the circuit. A&nbsp;
dangling piece of cable connected to nothing

00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:43.200
trimmed to the right length. And because the&nbsp;
stub is just more of the same cable, the fix

00:34:43.200 --> 00:34:48.080
is made of the thing that it's fixing. Now, the&nbsp;
series stub we've shown gets the idea across, but

00:34:48.080 --> 00:34:52.400
you'd rarely build one this way because a series&nbsp;
stub means cutting into your line. In practice,

00:34:52.400 --> 00:34:56.560
you'd rather just solder it in parallel. But&nbsp;
that introduces a new problem. In series,

00:34:56.560 --> 00:35:01.600
impedances add together cleanly. In parallel,&nbsp;
they don't, but one over impedance does. That's

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:06.400
admittance. The only catch is that admittance&nbsp;
curves the other way on the Smith chart. So,

00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:10.720
engineers use a flipped version of it called&nbsp;
the admittance Smith chart. Using this chart,

00:35:10.720 --> 00:35:16.240
matching stubs in parallel is easy. So, what&nbsp;
we've done today is basically what RF engineers

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:20.560
do every day. They play a game trying to zigzag&nbsp;
their way to the middle of the Smith chart.

00:35:21.760 --> 00:35:26.160
But all these charts have one big drawback.&nbsp;
They only work for a single frequency. But real

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:29.760
world signals aren't this perfect. The moment&nbsp;
you turn them on or change them in any way,

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:34.160
they spread across a range of frequencies. There&nbsp;
are even radio techniques that encode information

00:35:34.160 --> 00:35:39.680
by changing the frequency. I mean, you've probably&nbsp;
heard of them, frequency modulation or FM radio.

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:44.000
In practice, then the response of a system doesn't&nbsp;
just map to a single point on a chart, but rather

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:48.960
it traces out a curve across its frequency range.&nbsp;
So instead of matching one point on a Smith chart,

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:52.480
you try to bring that curve as close to the&nbsp;
center as possible over the range you care

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:58.000
about. So even then the chart is still useful.&nbsp;
So Phillip Smith designed a chart that could

00:35:58.000 --> 00:36:04.080
revolutionize how radio engineers worked. But at&nbsp;
first, no one really cared. It took about 2 years

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:09.360
of slow responses and rejections from technical&nbsp;
magazines before Electronics finally published

00:36:09.360 --> 00:36:13.760
Smith's chart. Engineers already had their own&nbsp;
methods, and this one required a completely

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:21.680
different way of thinking. At first, adoption&nbsp;
was slow, but World War II changed everything.

00:36:21.680 --> 00:36:26.080
In the Battle for the Atlantic, German U-boats&nbsp;
were sinking Allied ships faster than they could

00:36:26.080 --> 00:36:31.680
be replaced. At night or in bad weather, the&nbsp;
predators were almost impossible to find. At

00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:35.920
the MIT Radiation Laboratory, scientists&nbsp;
were building new microwave radar systems

00:36:35.920 --> 00:36:41.600
that could detect submarines on the surface, even&nbsp;
in complete darkness. And in the middle of a war,

00:36:41.600 --> 00:36:46.080
you don't have time for trial and error. You&nbsp;
need a fast, reliable way to fix these systems.

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:50.560
So Smith's chart was used constantly. When the&nbsp;
war ended, those same engineers took the chart

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:54.800
with them out into industry. Into the universities&nbsp;
they'd work in and the textbooks they'd go on to

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:59.840
write. Meanwhile, the other versions of the chart&nbsp;
by Mizuhashi and Volpert were used in Japan and

00:36:59.840 --> 00:37:04.800
the Soviet Union locally, but weren't spread&nbsp;
as widely. That's why today we refer to this

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:10.240
chart as the Smith chart. Now granted, engineers&nbsp;
don't need to impedance match by hand anymore.

00:37:10.240 --> 00:37:14.800
Computers can do it a lot faster. But this chart&nbsp;
is still taught around the world in engineering

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:20.240
classrooms and many engineers still rely on it&nbsp;
to this day. That's because while a computer

00:37:20.240 --> 00:37:25.360
can tell you the perfect match, it doesn't tell&nbsp;
you what to try or why it works. The Smith chart

00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:29.520
encodes that intuition. And because of that,&nbsp;
it's still a popular visualization tool in

00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:34.640
modern software. And it's used in almost all radio&nbsp;
frequency measurement tools. So, historically,

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:40.720
we would get a Smith chart and we'd use rulers and&nbsp;
we'd use protractors and compasses. These days,

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:45.600
we use a Smith chart more like a back of the&nbsp;
envelope calculation where it's like a map.

00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:50.800
It's like if if somebody asks you, "How do you get&nbsp;
to the nearest tube station?" You'd say, "Well,

00:37:50.800 --> 00:37:54.560
um, here's an envelope, you go down&nbsp;
the street here, turn left there,

00:37:54.560 --> 00:37:59.440
go further down there." And in the same&nbsp;
way, we use it to navigate from where

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:03.280
we are to where we want to be. Sure. So it&nbsp;
gives you an intuition of what direction to

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:08.080
go. That's right. Okay. Before the Smith chart,&nbsp;
engineers were stuck using cumbersome formulas

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:13.120
and guessing. It took a concerted effort by&nbsp;
Phillip Smith, Tosaku Mizuhashi, and Amiel

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:18.160
Volpert to make this complex world of reflections&nbsp;
and standing waves easier to understand. For me,

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:23.920
it brings to mind Mendeleev and the periodic table&nbsp;
or Feynman and his diagrams. The Smith chart is

00:38:23.920 --> 00:38:28.560
in that same vein. So much of scientific&nbsp;
progress comes not from new discoveries,

00:38:28.560 --> 00:38:34.000
but from new forms of representation. They make&nbsp;
hard problems easier to solve and make further

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:39.120
innovation and discovery possible. The Smith chart&nbsp;
helped advance radio frequency engineering. It

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:44.080
was crucial in building modern radar and sensing&nbsp;
systems. And the communication networks we rely on

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:54.960
today were no small part built from this chart.&nbsp;
So yes, it's scary, but it's also damn useful.

